items.body:
In React, each render sees a fixed snapshot of state. When an event handler calls a state setter with a function (`setX(current => ...)`), React passes that function the queued value. Other code in the same handler reads the variables captured when the component rendered. Knowing which value each line sees is the key skill for the quizzes on `App.jsx`.

The language-runtime guide has a "Failure modes" list for this repo. Read each item with its line anchor and, for each one, write down:

- which values in that handler come from the updater argument and which come from the render's closure;
- how the code makes ids for new rows and timeline events, and what React uses those ids for;
- for each `useMemo`, the exact dependency list, and which inputs the computation reads.

Then, for each item, construct one sequence of clicks that would expose it, and predict what the screen would show.
